      "title": "Relational data",
      "description": "Our data is relational. For example, users have analysis runs, those runs have recommendations, and each recommendation links to an asset.\n\nThat makes PostgreSQL and Supabase a good fit, because we can define those relationships using foreign keys and enforce rules through check constraints."
    },
    {
      "title": "Backend connection",
      "description": "The backend connects through a shared Supabase client. If the required connection settings are missing, it fails immediately, so we catch configuration problems at startup.",
      "file": "backend/src/utils/supabase_client.py",
      "line": 53
    },
    {
      "title": "Frontend connection",
      "description": "The frontend has a separate client configured with the public anon key. The privileged service-role key stays on the backend.",
      "file": "frontend/src/lib/supabase.ts",
      "line": 1
    },
    {
      "title": "Repository base and analysis-run repository",
      "description": "To organise database access, our main backend repositories share a common base class. This provides shared client and table access, and here’s the analysis-run repository extending it.",
      "file": "backend/src/utils/supabase_client.py",
      "line": 282
    },
    {
      "title": "Schema migrations",
      "description": "Our schema updates are recorded in numbered migration files, from 001 to 039, with headers explaining the reason for each change.",
